{"st":"TN","name":"Tennessee","seats":[{"census":1790,"seats":1},{"census":1800,"seats":3},{"census":1810,"seats":6},{"census":1820,"seats":9},{"census":1830,"seats":13},{"census":1840,"seats":11},{"census":1850,"seats":10},{"census":1860,"seats":8},{"census":1870,"seats":10},{"census":1880,"seats":10},{"census":1890,"seats":10},{"census":1900,"seats":10},{"census":1910,"seats":10},{"census":1920,"seats":10},{"census":1930,"seats":9},{"census":1940,"seats":10},{"census":1950,"seats":9},{"census":1960,"seats":9},{"census":1970,"seats":8},{"census":1980,"seats":9},{"census":1990,"seats":9},{"census":2000,"seats":9},{"census":2010,"seats":9},{"census":2020,"seats":9}],"events":[{"congress":4,"year":1796,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Tennessee became the 16th state on June 1, 1796. Congress gave it 1 seat in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"census","title":"1800 census apportionment","text":"After the 1800 census, Tennessee's House delegation grew from 1 to 3 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 8th Congress (1803–1805), Tennessee elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":13,"year":1812,"kind":"census","title":"1810 census apportionment","text":"After the 1810 census, Tennessee's House delegation grew from 3 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, Tennessee's House delegation grew from 6 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Tennessee's House delegation grew from 9 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Tennessee's House delegation shrank from 13 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Tennessee's House delegation shrank from 11 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Tennessee's House delegation shrank from 10 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Tennessee's House delegation grew from 8 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 43rd Congress (1873–1875), Tennessee elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Tennessee kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Tennessee kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Tennessee kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Tennessee kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Tennessee's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Tennessee's House delegation grew from 9 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Tennessee's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":87,"year":1962,"kind":"court","title":"Baker v. Carr","text":"Tennessee had not redrawn its legislative districts since 1901. The Supreme Court held that federal courts can hear claims that unequal districts violate the Equal Protection Clause, opening the way for \"one person, one vote\" rulings.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/369/186"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Tennessee kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Tennessee's House delegation shrank from 9 to 8 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Tennessee's House delegation grew from 8 to 9 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Tennessee kept 9 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Tennessee kept 9 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Tennessee kept 9 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Tennessee kept 9 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":120,"year":2026,"kind":"mid-decade","title":"Mid-decade redistricting","text":"In May 2026 the legislature passed a new congressional map that divided the Memphis-based 9th District among three districts, and the governor signed it on May 7. A federal court panel declined to block it in July 2026, and it was used in that year's election.","sources":["https://www.news4jax.com/news/politics/2026/07/23/federal-judges-allow-new-tennessee-congressional-map-that-splits-majority-black-district-in-memphis/"]}],"compactness":{"c004":{"TN-0":0.204},"c005":{"TN-0":0.204},"c006":{"TN-0":0.206},"c007":{"TN-0":0.206},"c008":{"TN-0":0.188},"c009":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.493,"TN-3":0.56},"c010":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.493,"TN-3":0.56},"c011":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.472,"TN-3":0.52},"c012":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.472,"TN-3":0.537},"c013":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.214,"TN-3":0.381,"TN-4":0.761,"TN-5":0.511,"TN-6":0.375},"c014":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.214,"TN-3":0.381,"TN-4":0.761,"TN-5":0.511,"TN-6":0.375},"c015":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.214,"TN-3":0.381,"TN-4":0.761,"TN-5":0.511,"TN-6":0.375},"c016":{"TN-1":0.439,"TN-2":0.214,"TN-3":0.381,"TN-4":0.761,"TN-5":0.511,"TN-6":0.375},"c017":{"TN-1":0.4